# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# BRIDGE — everything the bridge process itself reads, and nothing else.
#
# Admin credentials (superuser, and the CREATE ROLE passwords init.sql uses) live in
# .env.admin. They are deliberately not here: sourcing this file to launch the bridge
# should not put a superuser password in that shell.
#
#   # stack — .env.admin only: every service in it is an admin task (postgres bootstrap,
#   # init.sql, the NATS config and stream creation), and none of them is the bridge.
#   NATS_NKEY_SEED="SU..." docker compose -f docker-compose.full.yml \
#     --env-file .env.admin up -d
#
#   # bridge on the host, Postgres and NATS in Docker
#   set -a && source .env.bridge && set +a && \
#     NATS_NKEY_SEED="SU..." ./zig-out/bin/bridge --slot my_slot --pub my_pub
#
# `bridge --help` lists every variable it reads.
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# ─── Postgres, as the bridge connects to it ──────────────────────────────────
#
# The only way the bridge connects. There is no PG_HOST/PG_USER/PG_PASSWORD fallback
# any more: those are the superuser credentials init.sql runs under, and while they
# worked here, a missing or misspelled DATABASE_URL meant connecting as `postgres` and
# looking healthy. The bridge now refuses to start without this variable, and warns if
# it sees PG_HOST or PG_USER at all. `sslmode` goes in the query string.
#
# 127.0.0.1:55432 is the *published* port — the bridge runs on the host.

# Read path: SELECT + REPLICATION.
DATABASE_URL=postgres://bridge_reader:bridge_password_changeme@127.0.0.1:5432/postgres

# Ingress path. Unset disables the mutation listener entirely — deliberately, rather
# than falling back to the read role, which has REPLICATION.
DATABASE_WRITER_URL=postgres://bridge_writer:writer_password_changeme@127.0.0.1:5432/postgres

# ⚠️ The one value that has to agree across the two files: the port in the URLs above
# must match PG_PUBLISH_PORT in .env.admin, which is what compose actually publishes.
# It lives there because compose reads that file; setting it here would look effective
# and do nothing. Nothing at runtime can catch a mismatch — the bridge has no idea what
# compose published — so it is checked offline:
#
#   python scripts/scenarios/envcheck.py

# ─── NATS ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# NATS_URL is the only address input. NATS_HOST used to be a second one and is now
# ignored with a warning: it was read by the mutation listener alone, which then
# dialled NATS_HOST:4222 while everything else went where the URL said.

# Bridge on the host:
NATS_URL=nats://127.0.0.1:4222
# Bridge inside compose:
# NATS_URL=nats://nats-server:4222

# The bridge authenticates with the nkey SEED, passed on the command line and never
# stored here. Its public half (NATS_BRIDGE_NKEY_PUB) is what the *server* is configured
# to trust, so it lives in .env.admin with the other things that authorize rather than
# authenticate.
# NATS_NKEY_SEED=SU...   <-- pass in the CLI

# ─── Bridge runtime ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# HTTP telemetry port when --port is absent. --port wins when both are given.
BRIDGE_PORT=9090

LOG_LEVEL=debug

# Slot, for the compose bridge service's command line. The bridge binary itself takes it
# as --slot; it reads no env var for it.
#
# BRIDGE_CDC_PUBLICATION lives in .env.admin, not here: init.sql creates the publication,
# and one name in two files is one name too many.
#
# ⚠️ There is deliberately no table list. `BRIDGE_CDC_TABLES` used to sit here and was read
# by **nothing** — `replication_setup.zig` gets the monitored tables by querying
# `pg_publication_tables` for the publication, so the publication *is* the list. A second
# copy in an env file could only ever disagree with it, and disagree silently: the bridge
# would go on streaming whatever the publication said while this file claimed otherwise.
# Add or remove tables with `zebridge_enable()` / `ALTER PUBLICATION`, never here.
BRIDGE_CDC_SLOT=my_slot

# ─── Optional ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# Per-table columns for last-write-wins ingress.
# `<table>:<version>[,<tombstone>[,<tiebreak>]]`, semicolon-separated.
#
# These are YOUR column names; the payload field names are fixed by the protocol and never
# change (PROTOCOL.md §7.4):
#
#     test_types:updated_at,deleted_at,last_writer
#                ^version   ^tombstone ^tiebreak      ← your columns
#     payload:   "version"  (none)     "client_id"    ← the wire
#
# Only `test_types` is listed, and that is the point: it is the writable fixture (uuid PK
# the client mints, plus a write grant), while `users` is outbound-only. An unlisted table
# still gets the global default version column, so omitting `users` costs nothing — what
# makes it non-writable is its `bigserial` key and the absent grant, not this line.
#
# ⚠️ Every named column must **exist on the table**. Naming one that does not is not
# ignored: every mutation to that table fails `NoTombstoneColumn`, which is permanent, so
# it dead-letters on first delivery. (This line previously named `deleted_at` on
# `test_types` before the column existed.)
#
# The third field is the tiebreak column, and it is what makes equal versions *resolvable*
# rather than refused. Without it two writes carrying the same version are both rejected —
# which is not a resolution: two replicas each holding the other's row refuse each other
# forever, silently. With it, the higher `client_id` wins and is stored, so the comparison
# is `(version, client_id)`. Ties are the normal case for an integer version column and
# happen with timestamps inside the same microsecond. See `scripts/scenarios/tiebreak.py`.
#
# ⚠️ Read by the **sweeper** too (`bridge_sweeper` takes its sweep set from here), so the
# two cannot disagree about which column is the tombstone.
SYNC_RULES=test_types:updated_at,deleted_at,last_writer
# SYNC_VERSION_COLUMN=updated_at

TENANT_RULES=test_types:tenant_id

# Semantic event routing: when a monitored column changes, the event goes to
# cdc.<table>.update.transition instead of cdc.<table>.update.data. Tables without a
# rule keep the plain subject.
# TRANSITION_RULES=users:status,kyc_level;orders:state,payment_status
